WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:18.532
So I'm going to be talking about how we came to study

00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:21.266
genes for aging, or to ask whether there were genes for aging,

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:27.466
that is genes that affect aging. We started this work in the early 1990s, and at that time

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:31.932
people didn't think that aging was subject to any kind of control

00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:35.399
by the genes. They thought you just wear our like an old car wears out.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.632
There are several reasons why I thought maybe this would be wrong,

00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:41.366
that this idea was simplistic.

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:46.399
One is, that if you just look around in biology, nothing just happens.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.199
It seems like everything is regulated, everything seems to be

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.832
subject to control by the genes

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.299
and when you look at different species of animals,

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.732
you see that they can have really different lifespans.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:01.666
So the reason they have different lifespans is obviously because they have different genes.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:10.299
So that tells you right off the bat that genes have a lot to do with the lifespan of an animal

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.932
I had been working in the field of developmental biology,

00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:20.566
and during the time, during the early 1980s and 1990s it had become really clear

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:26.099
that basic fundamental mechanisms of life are conserved evolutionarily.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.332
I lived through this realization in the field of developmental biology

00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:36.332
when genes that pattern the body of worms, which is what we worked on

00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.066
were the same as genes that patterned the body of fruit flies and mice,

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:42.132
which was amazing because these animals look so different,

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.566
but there was a lot of commonality there.

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:47.032
The same thing was happening in other fields,

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:50.899
like the cell cycle is driven by the same kinds of genes in different kinds of animals.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:56.032
So it seemed that something so fundamental as aging, which seems to be evolvable

00:01:56.033 --> 00:02:00.066
in other words, there are different animals that have very different lifespans,

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:03.532
so evolution can definitely change the rate of aging.

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:06.366
So anyway, it seemed to me that there might be genes

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:10.299
that controlled aging, so to find those genes, if an animal has them, you can just

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.599
treat an animal with something that causes mutations and look for long lived mutants.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.732
We didn't study this in humans, instead we studied it in our little worms,

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:26.332
C. elegans, which is really nice for this kind of study because it is small, and simple,

00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:28.699
and it has a very rapid lifespan of just a few weeks.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.532
Actually, at the time we started our experiments,

00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:35.966
it was already known that there was a gene that affected the lifespan of C. elegans

00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:41.432
and if you change this gene, the animals could live about 50 percent longer than normal

00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:45.866
but there was a lot of mystery surrounding this gene, because these mutants for one thing

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:51.032
were not very fertile, and so evolutionary biologists thought that

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:53.699
it was possible that the reason they live long was that

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.699
they didn't have to channel all of their resources into reproduction,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.732
and so they would have more to live longer.

00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:02.932
But to me it seemed like maybe it wasn't such a simple trade-off,

00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:04.899
maybe there really were genes that controlled aging.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.366
And I should say one more thing, which is I was really fascinated by several things

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:12.799
that had been known for a long time, one was Progeria. There were human mutants

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.499
that aged much more quickly than normal, so that was really interesting to me

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.199
and the other thing was that there was a phenomenon

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.199
called the Hayflick limit, where you put cells in culture,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.399
they would just divide several times or a few number of times, and then they would stop dividing

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.966
and it had been reported that if you took the cells from a young person or animal,

00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:34.666
they divided more times than from an old individual,

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:38.866
and so there was some kind of intrinsic clock for aging. So it was such a fascinating field

00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:43.799
and it was discarded by molecular biologists, they just though there is nothing to study,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.299
and so we thought we would give it a try. So I then decided,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.899
okay well I'm going to look for genes that affect aging.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.399
We already had a lab working on C. elegans,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.832
they were studying development, but my lab members had no interest in aging,

00:03:56.833 --> 00:04:03.599
they thought it was a real kind of a, what's the word, a swamp, is that the word?

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.266
Anyway, not very interesting.  Most people had that idea because there was nothing to study.

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:13.199
Among molecular biologists, the field of aging had kind of bad reputation.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.299
So I couldn't get anyone to work on the project, and the idea was to look for long lived mutants

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.032
So I kept trying and trying, and there were these students

00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:24.632
called rotation students who come into UCSF, where I work, UCSF

00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:31.466
and before they join a lab to get their Ph.D. they spend their time in each of three labs.

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:34.799
So I tried to interest these students and they weren't interested.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.366
But then one student named Roman Tatiana thought finally

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:40.299
that it was a great idea, and he decided to look for long lived mutants.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.732
So amazingly enough he set out to look for long lived mutants and he found one

00:04:45.733 --> 00:04:50.499
He found that mutants that reduce the activity of a gene called daf-2 double the worm's lifespan.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.966
Here you see in black, you see the lifespan of a normal worm

00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:56.466
so by the end of 30 days, or about a month or so

00:04:56.467 --> 00:05:00.199
all the worms are dead. But you see in red, the daf-2 mutant,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.666
lives twice as long as normal, even more than twice as long.

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:08.699
The thing that was the most cool about this, was that the worms were, they didn't just go

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.899
into the nursing home and hang on, they were actually aging more slowly than normal

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.566
and I have a movie here to show you this.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:19.166
What this shows you first, is a normal worm, just to orient you.

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:24.032
So this is a young C. elegans adult, where it is about graduate student age

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:28.532
it's three days in worm time, and this is the long lived mutant when it is young,

00:05:28.533 --> 00:05:31.532
and the reason I am showing you this is that the mutant looks healthy

00:05:31.533 --> 00:05:35.599
and active, it looks great. And in fact these mutants can be completely fertile

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.599
so there is no reproductive trade-off involved here.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:46.366
Here is a normal worm where it is old. You can see that its head is moving here

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:50.066
but otherwise it looks like it is about to die, and it is about to die.

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:54.666
And now next you are going to see the long lived mutant at the exact same age

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:56.732
and what you'll see is that the mutant

00:05:56.733 --> 00:06:01.066
these are some more, a dead worm here and here is a worm in the nursing home

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:05.999
So now what you are going to see is at the same time, the long lived mutant looks much younger

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.332
than this, and I actually tell people about this, and they end up thinking

00:06:10.333 --> 00:06:13.599
oh it's like being 90 but looking really good when you're 90

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.799
but like a 90 year-old who is healthy. But it isn't and here is a little analogy

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.166
it's like this, suppose you are single and maybe in your 40's,

00:06:20.167 --> 00:06:22.932
and you are dating, and you find someone who you really like

00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:27.632
and you go on a couple of dates, and then you go with them to a restaurant

00:06:27.633 --> 00:06:31.132
and then you are sitting there with them and they ask well how old are you

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:34.199
and they say, oh, I'm 80, and you go oh-my-gosh because they look 40.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:41.266
That's what it is like, it's like they took two days to age as much as you normally age in one day.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:44.499
So it's something that, the reason I keep emphasizing this is that people

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:48.232
don't realize this is not something in our experience,

00:06:48.233 --> 00:06:50.199
we never thought this kind of thing could happen, but it did.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.832
We just change one  gene and we double the lifespan.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:55.599
So I have told you that was really hard to find anyone to work on this project

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.466
well Roman, the rotation student who just made this discovery

00:06:59.467 --> 00:07:02.066
didn't join our lab, he went to another lab

00:07:02.067 --> 00:07:04.599
and I still couldn't get anyone to work on the project

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.166
but there were all these experiments that we wanted to do with daf-2 mutants,

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:11.699
so we kept asking rotation students if they wanted to work on it, and they all did,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.432
they liked to work on it, so one after another they would come to the lab

00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:17.732
and they would do some more experiments on the daf-2 mutant,

00:07:17.733 --> 00:07:21.666
and finally we wrote a paper, and Nature published the paper,

00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:25.632
and except for me, every single person on the paper was a rotation student.

00:07:25.633 --> 00:07:28.899
The amazing thing is that not one of those rotation students joined the lab.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:35.499
They all went to other labs, and it was really a long time before anyone decided that aging was a

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.999
sure enough bet for a Ph.D. thesis that they would actually come to the lab and work on it.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.466
So what is this gene, what is this daf-2 gene?

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:48.299
Well the Gary Ruvkun lab cloned the gene, and they found that daf-2 encodes a hormone receptor

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.766
So that means hormones control aging, and not only that,

00:07:51.767 --> 00:07:56.832
it is similar to receptors that we knew about, number one, the human receptor for insulin,

00:07:56.833 --> 00:08:00.932
which is a hormone that controls nutrient uptake,

00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:05.099
and also it was similar to a hormone called IGF-1

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.032
which controls growth, and these hormones are similar to one another

00:08:08.033 --> 00:08:10.132
that is, it was similar to the receptor for these hormones.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:17.066
Okay, so what our findings had showed was that the worm's version of the same receptor

00:08:17.067 --> 00:08:20.132
had a third function, it also controlled aging

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:26.132
and we now know what that happens is when you slightly damage the daf-2 gene,

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:30.466
the animals sees it as a signal for danger so it

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:34.599
rolls out a protective stress response, and that's what makes it live longer.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.866
But anyway the big question then of course was is this just a worm thing,

00:08:37.867 --> 00:08:43.432
or is this also the case for higher organisms. And I'll just say really quickly

00:08:43.433 --> 00:08:49.332
that inhibiting this gene can extend the lifespan of flies, or mice, and there's a study of

00:08:49.333 --> 00:08:53.366
Ashkenazi Jews carried out by Nir Barzilai, and he showed that

00:08:53.367 --> 00:08:58.299
centenarians are more likely to have reduced function of the IGF-1 receptor gene

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:02.699
than are Ashkenazi Jews that die earlier. So at least in this population,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:08.366
this mutation in the same gene have been linked to exceptional longevity.

00:09:08.367 --> 00:09:12.699
We also found a gene called daf-16 is needed for the long lifespan,

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.432
and humans have this gene, and it's called FOX-O

00:09:15.433 --> 00:09:20.132
and it turns out that FOX-O variants are associated with exceptional longevity

00:09:20.133 --> 00:09:23.899
in populations all around the world. These little stars represent a population,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:30.899
showing that variants in this longevity gene are present in people who live a long time.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:39.099
So I think the bottom line here is that we started because we had an idea, I had an idea,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.966
that aging would be regulated. We were really really lucky to find the daf-2 gene,

00:09:42.967 --> 00:09:48.232
in fact my colleagues had said to me, one of them said, "You know, I knew people who started to

00:09:48.233 --> 00:09:50.632
work on aging and they just fell off the edge of the earth

00:09:50.633 --> 00:09:53.799
as if the earth was flat, they just fell of the edge of the earth",

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.466
but in this case we didn't fall off the edge of the earth, we found something really really interesting

00:09:58.467 --> 00:10:01.766
that may be conserved in humans, and actually these mutants

00:10:01.767 --> 00:10:05.799
are resistant to lots of diseases so there are all sorts of potential here for

00:10:05.800 --> 01:00:00.000
improving human health, but that's the story. Thank you.

